---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'About RaceCalc: Who We Are & Why These Tools Exist';
const description =
  'RaceCalc is an independent, sim-racer-run toolset for rig geometry and race planning: who builds it, how the calculators are tested, and how it stays free.';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'About' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} canonical="/about/">
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <h1>About RaceCalc</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      We build the sim racing calculators we kept wishing existed while setting up our own rigs:
      exact on the geometry, honest about the limits, free of clutter.
    </p>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="why">Why this site exists</h2>
      <p>
        FOV advice online swings between "set it to 45 and forget it" and spreadsheets that assume you
        know what "monitor distance" means. The underlying maths is simple: your screens are windows and
        the sim must project through them. RaceCalc turns that maths into fast, no-nonsense tools that run
        entirely in your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also noticed sim UIs hide their FOV behind inconsistent conventions, vertical here, horizontal
        there, an unbounded slider elsewhere, with no single place documenting which value to enter where.
        We keep that map on the tool pages, with the formulas behind every number published on the
        <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="how">How the tools are built &amp; tested</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Formulas from first principles.</strong> Projection maths applied to your physical measurements; no magic constants.</li>
        <li><strong>Tested against known values.</strong> Every formula has a hand-verified test case that must pass before deploy.</li>
        <li><strong>Client-side only.</strong> No build step sends your inputs anywhere; there is no server that could receive them.</li>
        <li><strong>Corrections are welcome and credited.</strong> If a constant or game note is wrong, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and we will fix it with a changelog entry.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="money">How the site makes money</h2>
      <p>
        Right now: nothing. RaceCalc is independent and self-funded. If we ever add sponsorships or
        affiliate links, they will be clearly labelled (<code>rel="sponsored"</code>) and will never
        affect recommendations. Tools stay free.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="people">Who runs it</h2>
      <p>
        A small crew of sim racers and software engineers. We run triples at home, spend too long
        measuring eye distances, and rebuild rigs more often than is reasonable.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        For corrections or partnership enquiries: <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> or {site.email}.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
